    <!-- Dialogs -->
    <Dialog id="knobs" title="Joel's strategy, as knobs" eyebrow="Strategy" wide>
      <p>The default is the guide's RB-heavy script: RB/RB, three RBs from the top ~30, WR in rounds 3 and 5, QB in the QB7-11 band, TE only at true best-available, no K or DST until the last two rounds. Every rule is a tunable field on a dataclass, not hard-coded behaviour, so you can overrule it after a mock and the change persists per league.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Knob</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{knobs.map((k) => <tr><td><code>{k.k}</code></td><td class="num">{k.v}</td><td>{k.why}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Six presets</h3>
      <ul class="dc-presets" role="list">
        {presets.map((pr) => <li><code>{pr.name}</code>{pr.tag && <span class="chip chip--accent">{pr.tag}</span>}<span>{pr.text}</span></li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted">Superflex and dynasty leagues auto-select their preset; 8-team and 16-team leagues get their scarcity weights rescaled. From the terminal: <code>ffbot strategy set --name joel_rb_heavy rb_target_count 2</code>. In chat, "stop fading the RB dead zone so hard" becomes a <code>set_strategy</code> call.</p>
    </Dialog>

    <Dialog id="guide" title="What it learned from the guide" eyebrow="Data">
      <p><code>scripts/parse_guide.py</code> turns the PDF of <a href={guideUrl}>Joel Smyth's 2026 Draft Guide</a> (last update {draft.guide.lastUpdate}) into JSON. The extraction was cross-checked: the big boards and the positional lists agree with each other on 7 of 8 lists with zero mismatches.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Extracted</th><th scope="col">Detail</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{guideRows.map(([k, v]) => <tr><td>{k}</td><td>{v}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout callout--warn"><b>Not extracted:</b> the DST and kicker pages, OL rankings, playcaller table, research charts and player cards are images with no text layer. The bot therefore has no DST or kicker rankings of its own and says so: it treats defences as interchangeable and tells you to check the guide's page 9.</div>
      <p class="small muted">The per-player "reason" annotations in the adjusted PPG tables sit in a separate PDF column with no recoverable row alignment, so they are kept as page-level context, not per player.</p>
    </Dialog>

    <Dialog id="extension" title="The extension is a viewer, and only a viewer" eyebrow="Chrome extension">
      <p>A browser extension on a draft page could do far more than this one does, so the limits are explicit. It does not read, parse or scrape the draft page. It does not click anything, autopick or submit a pick. It does not send your Sleeper data anywhere. All it injects is a container with an iframe pointing at the panel; the bot learns about the draft the way it always has, by polling Sleeper's public API from Python. Pull the plug on the extension and nothing about the advice changes.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>storage</code></td><td>Remembers the port, sidebar width and collapsed state, locally.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>localhost</code></td><td>Lets the service worker check whether your panel is running.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Content script on sleeper.com, sleeper.app and fantasysports.yahoo.com</td><td>Mounts the sidebar container inside draft rooms, in a shadow root, and nothing else.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Manifest V3. No <code>tabs</code>, <code>webRequest</code> or <code>scripting</code> permission and no access to any other site. The panel server answers Chrome's private-network preflight only for Sleeper's origins; any other site gets a 403. Drag the left edge to resize between 320 and 900 px, hide collapses it to a pill, and press <code>/</code> inside it to jump to the chat box.</p>
      <p class="small muted">Honest testing note from the repository: the server side of the contract (frame-ancestors, the preflight, origin refusal, token enforcement) is covered by automated tests; the packaged extension itself had not been exercised in a packaged Chrome profile in the environment it was built in, so the first load is treated as a smoke test.</p>
    </Dialog>

    <Dialog id="mcp" title="The 18 MCP tools" eyebrow="Claude plugin">
      <p><code>.mcp.json</code> registers an MCP server, so during a draft you talk to Claude instead of learning a CLI: "Connect to my draft at sleeper.com/draft/nfl/123456, I'm sidharth", "Who should I take?", "Why not Bhayshul Tuten?", "Compare Tuten and Hampton", "What's slot 8 doing?", "I want to go zero-RB from here". Claude keeps the draft session open across the whole conversation, so it always has your roster, the board and the room read in context.</p>
      <ul class="dc-tools" role="list">{mcpTools.map((t) => <li><code>{t}</code></li>)}</ul>
      <p class="small muted">Every tool returns the same state blob the web panel uses, so Claude and the panel never disagree about the board.</p>
    </Dialog>
  </div>

